<article class="wrap">

  <p>Every interview setup lives or dies on one lens choice. Lighting can be fixed in five minutes, audio can be cleaned up in post, but a wrong focal length on a talking head is baked into the shot forever — you cannot un-distort a nose that got stretched by a 24mm lens two feet from someone's face. This guide covers the exact focal length range that flatters faces, the aperture you actually need for background separation without losing focus on a moving subject, and specific lens recommendations across three budget tiers.</p>

  <div class="callout"><b>Quick take:</b> For a single subject sitting reasonably still, an 85mm f/1.8 (full-frame equivalent) is the gold standard for interviews — flattering compression, creamy background blur, sharp focus falloff. If your subject moves around while talking (hand gestures, leaning in and out), drop to a 50mm f/1.8-f/2.8 so your focus plane has more breathing room. Budget shooters should start with a 50mm f/1.8 "nifty fifty" — it is the single best value lens for this job at any price.</div>

  <h2>Why 50mm-85mm Is the Sweet Spot for Faces</h2>
  <p>Facial perspective is controlled by camera-to-subject distance, not focal length directly — but focal length determines how close you need to stand to fill the frame the same way, and that distance is what actually distorts or flatters a face.</p>
  <p>Shoot an interview at 24mm and you have to get uncomfortably close to fill the frame with a face. At that distance, the parts of the face nearer the lens (nose, forehead) are proportionally much closer to the sensor than the parts further away (ears, back of the head), and the lens exaggerates that difference. The result is a stretched, bulging look that nobody wants in a talking-head video.</p>
  <p>Move out to 50mm-85mm and you can frame a tight headshot from a comfortable 5-10 feet away. At that working distance, the difference in distance between the nose and the ears becomes proportionally small, so the perspective compresses and flattens naturally. This is the same optical principle that makes classic portrait photography lean on 85mm and 105mm lenses. Anything from 50mm up through 135mm will read as flattering; 50mm-85mm is simply the practical sweet spot because it still lets you shoot in a normal-sized room without backing into a wall.</p>
  <div class="callout">If you want the full optical breakdown of why focal length changes facial proportions — with visual comparisons at 24mm, 35mm, 50mm, and 85mm — we cover it in detail in our <a href="/focal-length-perspective-facial-distortion-portraits">focal length and facial distortion guide</a>.</div>

  <h3>What Happens Below 50mm</h3>
  <p>A 35mm lens is usable for interviews if you are shooting two people in a wider two-shot, or if your room is genuinely too small for anything longer. But for a single tight-to-medium headshot, 35mm starts to add a subtle wideness to the face that most viewers will notice even if they cannot name why the shot feels "off."</p>

  <h3>What Happens Above 85mm</h3>
  <p>100mm-135mm lenses give even more compression and a genuinely gorgeous, ultra-flat look — the classic beauty-portrait perspective. The tradeoff is practical: you need 10-15 feet of working distance to frame correctly, which most home studios, offices, and small sets simply do not have. Reserve these focal lengths for bigger rooms or when you are shooting with a longer zoom on a tripod at the back of a space.</p>

  <h2>Aperture: Background Separation Without Losing the Shot</h2>
  <p>The aperture question in interview work is really a tradeoff between two things creators want simultaneously: a soft, blurred background (which needs a wide aperture) and reliable focus on a subject who inevitably leans forward, turns their head, or gestures with their whole upper body (which needs a deeper depth of field to forgive small movements).</p>
  <p>Here is the practical range that works:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>f/1.4-f/1.8</strong> — Maximum background blur, best for a subject who sits still and stays roughly the same distance from the lens (studio interviews, podcast-style setups with a fixed chair). Depth of field at these apertures on a tight headshot can be just a few inches, so any real forward-and-back movement will soften focus unless you are using continuous autofocus with reliable face/eye tracking.</li>
    <li><strong>f/2-f/2.8</strong> — The safest working range for most real-world interviews. Still gives a clearly separated, soft background, but gives you enough depth of field cushion that natural head movement, hand gestures, and breathing don't push the subject out of focus. This is where most professional interview work actually lives.</li>
    <li><strong>f/4 and narrower</strong> — Only worth using if you are lighting a busy or messy background you want to keep somewhat readable (a bookshelf, a workshop, a branded backdrop with logos), or if you are shooting outdoors in bright daylight without ND filters and need to stop down to avoid overexposure.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout"><b>Pro tip:</b> If your subject moves a lot — leans in for emphasis, turns to gesture at something off camera — shoot at f/2.8 instead of f/1.4 and let your <a href="/depth-of-field-explained-bokeh-guide">depth of field</a> do the forgiving. A slightly less blurred background that stays in focus always looks more professional than a gorgeous bokeh ball where the subject's eyes have drifted soft.</div>

  <h2>Autofocus vs. Manual Focus for Talking Heads</h2>
  <p>Unlike a static product shot, a talking head is a moving target — even a "still" subject sways forward and back by a few inches while speaking. Two workable approaches:</p>
  <h3>1. Reliable Eye-AF with Continuous Autofocus</h3>
  <p>Most mirrorless cameras from the last few years have genuinely good human eye-detection autofocus. If your camera and lens combination has fast, sticky eye-AF, this is the easiest and safest way to shoot interviews solo — it will hold focus through natural movement even at f/1.8.</p>
  <h3>2. Manual Focus with a Focus Assistant</h3>
  <p>If you are on an older camera, a manual-focus cine lens, or an autofocus system you don't fully trust, stop down slightly (f/2.8-f/4) to buy yourself a deeper focus plane, mark your subject's chair with tape, and pull focus manually or lock it once they are seated. This is the traditional documentary approach and it is completely reliable once your subject agrees to stay roughly in one spot.</p>

