    <p>Nearly every camera brand makes a 50mm f/1.8 for well under the price of any zoom lens, and it is genuinely the best starting point for interview work. On a full-frame body it gives you the classic "normal" focal length with a comfortably flattering perspective at typical sit-down distances; on an APS-C or Micro Four Thirds body, the crop factor pushes the equivalent field of view closer to 75-100mm, which if anything makes it even better suited to tight headshot interviews.</p>
    <p><strong>DOP's take:</strong> This is the lens I tell every beginner to buy first, full stop. At f/1.8 it separates a subject from a background beautifully, autofocus is fast because the optical design is simple, and because it's a fixed focal length there is no zoom creep or breathing to worry about mid-interview. The only real limitation is that on a full-frame body you need roughly 6-8 feet of distance to get a tight headshot, so make sure your room has that much depth.</p>
