      <span class="spec-chip">Heavier, tripod-recommended</span>
    </div>
    <p>Once you are running a proper multi-camera interview setup — a wide two-shot camera plus a tight single on the subject, or a documentary-style production where you need to reframe without walking across the room — a fast zoom earns its keep. A 24-70mm f/2.8 covers everything from a wider establishing frame down into flattering headshot range, while a 70-200mm f/2.8 lets you sit a camera far back from the subject (useful in small studios where you can't physically stand at 85mm-equivalent distance) and still pull in tight.</p>
    <p><strong>DOP's take:</strong> This is the setup I use on paid shoots where reframing on the fly matters more than squeezing the absolute maximum bokeh out of a prime. f/2.8 is genuinely enough depth of field cushion for interview work, and the ability to zoom between a medium shot and a tight closeup without swapping lenses or repositioning the camera saves real time on a shoot day. It is a bigger investment, and these lenses are noticeably heavier, so plan on a solid tripod or gimbal rather than handholding for a full interview session.</p>
    <div class="pc-grid">
